{"toolCallId":"call_a30d3d1414294c779d3b2f31fe80ef79","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"14:# Level Specifications - Complete Level 1-3+ Requirements\n15-\n16-Complete specifications for all documentation levels using the manifest-backed Level contract architecture where higher levels add value, not just length.\n17-\n18----\n19-\n20-## 1. OVERVIEW\n21-\n22-### Template Architecture: Manifest-Backed Level Contracts\n23-\n24-Templates use a manifest-backed model where `templates/spec-kit-docs.json` selects rendered `*.md.tmpl` documents for each Level:\n25-\n26-```\n27-templates/\n28-├── spec-kit-docs.json       # Level contract and document membership\n29-├── spec.md.tmpl             # Requirements and scope\n30-├── plan.md.tmpl             # Technical approach\n31-├── tasks.md.tmpl            # Task breakdown\n32-├── acceptance-criteria.md.tmpl  # Closure criteria (Level 2+)\n33-├── decision-record.md.tmpl  # Architecture decisions\n34-├── implementation-summary.md.tmpl\n35-├── phase-parent.spec.md.tmpl\n36-├── handover.md.tmpl\n37-├── debug-delegation.md.tmpl\n38-├── resource-map.md.tmpl\n39-└── research.md.tmpl          # Optional research notes\n40-```\n41-\n42-### Template Paths - Quick Reference\n43-\n44-| Path | Purpose | When to Use |\n--\n55:Level 1 (Core):         Essential what/why/how (~455 LOC)\n56-         ↓ +Verify\n57-Level 2 (Verification): +Quality gates, NFRs, edge cases (~875 LOC)\n58-         ↓ +Arch\n59-Level 3 (Full):         +Architecture decisions, ADRs, risk matrix (~1090 LOC)\n60-         ↓ +Govern\n61-Level 3+ (Extended):    +Enterprise governance, AI protocols (~1075 LOC)\n62-```\n63-\n64-### What Each Level ADDS (Value Scaling)\n65-\n66-| Level | Adds | New Content |\n67-|-------|------|-------------|\n68-| **L1 (Core)** | Essential what/why/how | Problem, scope, requirements, success criteria |\n69-| **L2 (+Verify)** | Quality gates | NFRs, edge cases, checklist, effort estimation |\n70-| **L3 (+Arch)** | Architecture decisions | Executive summary, ADRs, risk matrix, milestones |\n71-| **L3+ (+Govern)** | Enterprise governance | Approval workflow, compliance, AI protocols |\n72-\n73-**Key Points:**\n74-- LOC thresholds are **SOFT GUIDANCE** (not enforcement)\n75-- **Higher levels add VALUE** - not just more boilerplate\n76-- **Verification is script-assisted** - use `validate.sh`, `check-completion.sh`, and `check-placeholders.sh` where applicable\n77-- When in doubt, choose higher level\n78-\n79-**Note:** Single typo/whitespace fixes (<5 characters in one file) are exempt from spec folder requirements.\n80-\n81----\n82-\n83-## 2. LEVEL 1: BASELINE DOCUMENTATION (LOC GUIDANCE: <100)\n84-\n85-### When to Use\n--\n128:**Good fits for Level 1:**\n129-- Fix \"Error\" → \"Eror\" typo\n130-- Update comment for clarity\n131-- Add email validation to contact form\n132-- Fix bug in calculation logic\n133-- Add loading spinner to form submission\n134-- Update error message formatting\n135-- Add new API endpoint (simple CRUD)\n136-- Refactor single component for clarity\n137-\n138-**Escalate to Level 2 if:**\n139-- Needs systematic QA validation\n140-- Affects multiple systems (not localized)\n141-- High risk (security, config cascades)\n142-- LOC estimate increases to 100+\n143-\n144-### Template Sources\n145-\n146-**Manifest templates (scaffolded by `create.sh`):**\n147-- `templates/core/spec.md.tmpl`\n148-- `templates/core/plan.md.tmpl`\n149-- `templates/core/tasks.md.tmpl`\n150-- `templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl`\n151-\n152-**Core source (for reference):**\n153-- `templates/core/spec.md.tmpl`\n154-- `templates/core/plan.md.tmpl`\n155-- `templates/core/tasks.md.tmpl`\n156-- `templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl`\n157-\n158-### Template Adaptation\n--\n183:### Required Files (Level 1 + checklist)\n184-\n185:- `spec.md` (from Level 1) - Requirements and user stories\n186:- `plan.md` (from Level 1) - Technical implementation plan\n187:- `tasks.md` (from Level 1) - Task breakdown by user story\n188:- `implementation-summary.md` (from Level 1) - Post-implementation documentation\n189-- `acceptance-criteria.md` (required at Level 2) - the criteria that decide whether the packet may close\n190-\n191-### Optional Files\n192-\n193-- `resource-map.md` - lean path catalog (cross-cutting, see §9)\n194-\n195-### Content Expectations\n196-\n197:**All Level 1 content expectations PLUS:**\n198-\n199-**acceptance-criteria.md required sections:**\n200-- Pre-implementation validation\n201-- Per-task verification\n202-- Integration testing steps\n203-- Security review checklist (if applicable)\n204-- Deployment verification\n205-\n206-**Enforcement:** Hard block if `acceptance-criteria.md` missing\n207-\n208-### Example Scenarios\n209-\n210-**Good fits for Level 2:**\n211-- Create reusable modal component with animations\n212-- Implement form validation framework\n213-- Add authentication flow\n214-- Migrate from library A to library B\n215-- Build file upload feature with progress tracking\n216-- Refactor state management approach\n217-- Config changes affecting multiple systems\n218-- Security-related changes\n219-\n220-**Escalate to Level 3 if:**\n221-- Requires architectural decisions to be documented\n222-- Discover >500 LOC during implementation\n223-- Complexity increases substantially\n224-- Need multiple developers for coordination\n225-- Architectural impact broader than anticipated\n226-\n227-### Template Sources\n--\n243:**All Level 1 adaptations PLUS:**\n244-\n245-**acceptance-criteria.md:**\n246-1. Fill pre-implementation checks specific to feature\n247-2. Add implementation validation steps\n248-3. Define testing checklist items\n249-4. Include deployment verification steps\n250-5. Add security checks if applicable\n251-6. Remove all sample content\n252-\n253-### Level 2 Example: API Endpoint Feature\n254-\n255-**Scenario:** Adding a new REST API endpoint (~200 LOC)\n256-\n257-**Folder Structure:**\n258-```\n259-012-user-profile-api/\n260-├── spec.md                    # Requirements and scope\n261-├── plan.md                    # Technical approach\n262-├── tasks.md                   # Implementation tasks\n263-├── implementation-summary.md  # Post-implementation outcomes\n264-├── acceptance-criteria.md     # Closure criteria (Level 2+)\n265-├── description.json           # Generated packet metadata\n266-├── graph-metadata.json        # Generated graph metadata\n267-└── scratch/                   # Temporary files\n268-```\n269-\n270-**Checklist Example:**\n271-```markdown\n272-## Implementation Checklist\n273-\n--\n579:- 2025-11-15: Created as Level 1 (simple feature) - spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md\n580-- 2025-11-16: Escalated to Level 2 (discovered validation needs) - added acceptance-criteria.md\n581-- 2025-11-17: Escalated to Level 3 (architectural decision required) - added decision-record.md\n582-```\n583-\n584-**Rules:**\n585-- Keep existing documentation (progressive enhancement - don't delete lower-level files)\n586-- Update `level:` field in metadata\n587-- Document reason for escalation\n588-- Inform user of level change and implications\n589-\n590-### When to Stay at Current Level\n591-\n592-**Don't escalate unnecessarily:**\n593:- Minor scope increase (50 → 95 LOC still Level 1)\n594-- Complexity didn't actually increase (just took longer than expected)\n595-- One additional file doesn't change coordination needs\n596-\n597-**Stability preferred:**\n598-- Once started, try to stay at chosen level\n599-- Only escalate if genuinely needed\n600-- Inform user before escalating\n601-\n602----\n603-\n604-## 7. STATUS FIELD CONVENTION\n605-\n606-Every spec.md should include a status field to track lifecycle:\n607-\n608-```yaml\n609----\n610-title: Feature Name\n611-created: 2025-11-15\n612-status: active  # ← Add this field\n613-level: 2\n614----\n615-```\n616-\n617-### Valid Status Values\n618-\n619-| Status     | Meaning                 | When to Use                        | Reuse Priority              |\n620-| ---------- | ----------------------- | ---------------------------------- | --------------------------- |\n621-| `draft`    | Planning phase          | Initial spec creation, not started | 2 (can start)               |\n622-| `active`   | Work in progress        | Currently implementing             | 1 (highest - continue here) |\n623-| `paused`   | Temporarily on hold     | Blocked or deprioritized           | 3 (can resume)              |\n--\n659:- Example: Level 1 bug fix → Requires Level 2 refactor → Add plan.md to same folder\n660-\n661-✅ **Feature enhancement** - Adding to existing functionality\n662-- Example: \"Add dark mode to modal\" → Update modal-component spec\n663-\n664-✅ **Resuming paused work** - Continuing previously paused implementation\n665-- Example: Spec status: paused → active (add continuation notes)\n666-\n667-### When to CREATE New Spec\n668-\n669-Create a new spec folder when:\n670-\n671-❌ **Distinct feature** - Completely separate functionality\n672-- Example: \"markdown-c7-optimizer\" ≠ \"markdown-validator\" (different purposes)\n673-\n674-❌ **Different approach** - Alternative implementation strategy\n675-- Example: \"hero-animation-css\" vs \"hero-animation-js\" (different approaches)\n676-\n677-❌ **Separate user story** - Different requirement or use case\n678-- Example: \"user-authentication\" ≠ \"user-profile\" (separate stories)\n679-\n680-❌ **Complete redesign** - Starting over with new architecture\n681-- Example: \"payment-flow-v2\" (complete rewrite of v1)\n682-\n683-❌ **Unrelated work** - No connection to existing specs\n684-- Example: \"add-search-feature\" ≠ \"fix-form-validation\" (different areas)\n685-\n686-### Decision Flowchart\n687-\n688-```\n689-User requests modification\n--\n744:- Level 1: `templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl`\n745-- Level 2: `templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl`\n746-- Level 3: `templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl`\n747-- Level 3+: `templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl`\n748-- `templates/addons/resource-map.md.tmpl`\n749-\n750-**Why Required for ALL Levels:**\n751-- `implementation-summary.md` documents outcomes, lessons learned, and deviations from plan\n752-- Ensures proper documentation trail for future sessions, handoffs, and audits\n753:- Even simple Level 1 tasks benefit from documenting what was actually done\n754-- Each level's template is progressively more detailed to match the complexity expectations\n755-\n756-### Canonical Continuity (Not Templates)\n757-\n758-| Surface | Purpose | Creation Method |\n759-|---------|---------|-----------------|\n760-| `_memory.continuity` in `implementation-summary.md` | Thin packet continuity state for resume and handoff recovery | `generate-context.js` runtime script via `/speckit:save` |\n761-| `scratch/` | Temporary workspace (disposable) | Manual creation (no template needed) |\n762-\n763-**Important:**\n764-- Canonical continuity is script-managed and should NOT be authored manually\n765-- Use `/speckit:save` or `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js --json '{\"specFolder\":\"...\",\"sessionSummary\":\"...\"}' specs/###-folder/`\n766-- Recovery should follow `handover.md -> _memory.continuity -> spec docs`\n767-- Scratch folder contents are temporary and should be cleaned up after work completes\n768-\n769----\n770-\n771-## 10. PHASE-AWARE SPECIFICATIONS\n772-\n773-### Phases as a Behavioral Overlay\n774-\n775-Phases are a **behavioral overlay** on the existing level system, not a new level tier. Any documentation level can technically use phases, though Level 3 and Level 3+ specifications are the most common candidates due to their inherent complexity.\n776-\n777-### How Phases Interact with Levels\n778-\n779-| Level | Phase Usage | Typical Scenario |\n780-|-------|-------------|-----------------|\n781:| Level 1 | Rare | Simple tasks rarely need decomposition |\n782-| Level 2 | Uncommon | Moderate complexity usually fits one folder |\n783-| Level 3 | Common | Complex features benefit from parallel work streams |\n784-| Level 3+ | Very common | Enterprise-scale work often requires phase decomposition |\n785-\n786-### Phase Detection Thresholds\n787-\n788-Phase decomposition is suggested when BOTH conditions are met:\n789-- Complexity score >= 25 (from 5-dimension scoring)\n790-- Documentation level >= 3\n791-\n792-A series parent is the one exception. It groups separate small packets that change the same artifact in the same track, and it qualifies without these thresholds. See `../structure/phase-definitions.md` §2.\n793-\n794-### What Phases Add to a Level\n795-\n796-Phases do not change the documentation requirements for a given level. Each phase child folder independently follows the level requirements:\n797-- A Level 3 parent with phases produces Level 3 child folders (each with spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md, acceptance-criteria.md, decision-record.md)\n798-- The parent folder adds a **Phase Documentation Map** section to its spec.md\n799-- Child folders add a **parent back-reference** to their spec.md metadata\n800-\n801-### Full Documentation\n802-\n803-See [phase-definitions.md](../structure/phase-definitions.md) for complete phase system documentation including scoring dimensions, folder structure, lifecycle, and validation rules.\n804-\n805----\n806-\n807-## 11. RELATED RESOURCES\n808-\n809-### Reference Files\n810-- [quick-reference.md](../workflows/quick-reference.md) - Commands, checklists, and troubleshooting\n811-- [template-guide.md](./template-guide.md) - Template selection, adaptation, and quality standards\n--\n823:**Composed Level 1 (Core only ~455 LOC):**\n824-- [spec template](../../templates/core/spec.md.tmpl) - Requirements and scope\n825-- [plan.md.tmpl](../../templates/core/plan.md.tmpl) - Technical plan\n826-- [tasks.md.tmpl](../../templates/core/tasks.md.tmpl) - Task breakdown\n827-- [implementation-summary.md.tmpl](../../templates/core/implementation-summary.md.tmpl) - Outcomes\n828-\n829-**Level 2 (verification):**\n830:- All Level 1 templates + quality gates, NFRs\n831-- [acceptance-criteria.md.tmpl](../../templates/addons/acceptance-criteria.md.tmpl) - The closure gate\n832-\n833-**Level 3 (architecture):**\n834-- All Level 2 templates + architecture decisions\n835-- [decision-record.md.tmpl](../../templates/addons/decision-record.md.tmpl) - ADRs\n836-- [research.md.tmpl](../../templates/addons/research.md.tmpl) - Optional research template copied into `research/research.md`\n837-\n838-**Level 3+ (governance):**\n839-- All Level 3 templates + approval workflow, compliance, stakeholders\n840-- Extended checklist with approval tracking\n841-\n842-**Session Management Templates (Any Level):**\n843-- [handover.md.tmpl](../../templates/addons/handover.md.tmpl) - Session context transfer\n844-- [debug-delegation.md.tmpl](../../templates/addons/debug-delegation.md.tmpl) - Debug task delegation\n845-- [resource-map.md.tmpl](../../templates/addons/resource-map.md.tmpl) - Optional lean path catalog\n846-\n847-**Non-Template Helpers:**\n848-- canonical continuity inside packet docs, primarily `_memory.continuity` in `implementation-summary.md`\n849-- `scratch/` - Temporary workspace (create ad-hoc files as needed)\n850-\n851-### Related Skills\n852-- `sk-code` - Implementation, debugging, and verification lifecycle\n853-- `system-spec-kit` - Context preservation with semantic memory\n854-- `sk-git` - Git workspace setup and clean commits\n855-\n856----\n"}}],"_meta":{"cognition.ai/cwd":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/wa3f8a76d29/tree","terminal_exit":{"terminal_id":"e3da98","exit_code":0,"signal":null},"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec"}}
